WEBVTT

00:00:02.647 --> 00:00:08.887
And I started from like the ground up where I knew nothing and I was building

00:00:08.587 --> 00:00:10.947
something to where I feel like...

00:00:10.647 --> 00:00:11.967
Like you literally built the machine.

00:00:12.174 --> 00:00:15.194
And so I had to understand.

00:00:15.114 --> 00:00:19.874
I think like people coming into the lab in a semester when things have been

00:00:19.574 --> 00:00:23.527
built, and they're just kind of like, I never got the chance to learn any

00:00:23.227 --> 00:00:23.600
of this.

00:00:23.887 --> 00:00:30.027
And then the feedback cycle can become, that didn't work, try again.

00:00:29.947 --> 00:00:30.367
Yeah.

00:00:30.347 --> 00:00:31.700
That didn't work, try again.

00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:39.533
AI could write the first draft of a paper that then we read and really understand

00:00:39.786 --> 00:00:44.226
Xavier was explaining the Bayesian optimization to Seth and I was listening

00:00:43.926 --> 00:00:49.526
in and I could tell that Xavier really understood that he wasn't just using

00:00:49.226 --> 00:00:50.267
AI to do the learning
